### What is @Scheduled and how do fixedRate, fixedDelay, and cron differ?

@Scheduled runs a method on a timer. You must annotate a @Configuration class with @EnableScheduling to activate it. There are three scheduling modes, and understanding the difference is critical for avoiding overlapping executions.

- **fixedRate** fires at a constant interval regardless of how long the previous execution takes. If the method takes 10 seconds and fixedRate is 5 seconds, the next execution starts immediately after the previous one finishes (it was already due).
- **fixedDelay** waits a specified time after the previous execution completes before starting the next one. There is never overlap.
- **cron** uses a calendar expression to fire at specific times.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh uses cron to calculate interest at 2 AM daily, and fixedDelay to poll the payment gateway every 30 seconds without overlap.

```java
package com.bank.scheduler;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Component
public class InterestScheduler {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(InterestScheduler.class);

    // Cron: runs at 2 AM every day
    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void calculateDailyInterest() {
        log.info("Starting daily interest calculation for all savings accounts");
        // calculate interest for each account using BigDecimal
    }

    // fixedDelay: waits 30 seconds AFTER previous run completes
    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 30000)
    public void pollPaymentGateway() {
        log.info("Polling payment gateway for pending settlements");
        // no overlap guaranteed
    }

    // fixedRate: fires every 60 seconds regardless of duration
    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 60000)
    public void refreshExchangeRates() {
        log.info("Refreshing exchange rates from external API");
        // may overlap if the previous run takes more than 60 seconds
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableScheduling;

@Configuration
@EnableScheduling  // Required -- without this, all @Scheduled annotations are ignored
public class SchedulingConfig {
}
```

```text
fixedRate vs fixedDelay timeline (rate/delay = 5s, task takes 8s):

fixedRate:
  |--task1 (8s)--|--task2 (8s)--|--task3 (8s)--|
  0s             8s            16s            24s
  (task2 was due at 5s, starts immediately at 8s)

fixedDelay:
  |--task1 (8s)--|  5s  |--task2 (8s)--|  5s  |--task3--|
  0s             8s     13s            21s    26s
  (always waits 5s after previous completes)
```

### What is @Retryable and how does exponential backoff work?

@Retryable from spring-retry retries a method when it throws a specified exception. You configure the maximum number of attempts and the backoff delay. When all retries are exhausted, the @Recover method provides a fallback.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh's dividend payout service calls the core banking API, which sometimes fails with transient network errors. @Retryable retries 3 times with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s) before falling back to a manual processing queue.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Recover;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Retryable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class DividendPayoutService {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(DividendPayoutService.class);

    @Retryable(
        retryFor = java.net.ConnectException.class,
        maxAttempts = 3,
        backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000, multiplier = 2)
    )
    public String payDividend(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        log.info("Attempting dividend payout: {} to account {}", amount, accountId);
        return coreBankingClient.creditAccount(accountId, amount);
        // Attempt 1: fails -> wait 1s
        // Attempt 2: fails -> wait 2s
        // Attempt 3: fails -> @Recover kicks in
    }

    @Recover
    public String fallback(java.net.ConnectException ex, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        log.warn("All retries exhausted for account {}. Queuing for manual processing.", accountId);
        manualQueue.enqueue(accountId, amount);
        return "QUEUED_FOR_MANUAL_PROCESSING";
    }
}
```

```java
