```

### What is Testcontainers and how do you use it for integration tests?

Testcontainers starts real Docker containers during integration tests. Instead of using an in-memory database like H2 (which behaves differently from PostgreSQL), Rajesh tests against a real PostgreSQL container that matches production.

Spring Boot 3.1+ supports @ServiceConnection, which auto-configures the DataSource to point at the Testcontainers instance.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.jdbc.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.orm.jpa.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.testcontainers.service.connection.ServiceConnection;
import org.testcontainers.containers.PostgreSQLContainer;
import org.testcontainers.junit.jupiter.Container;
import org.testcontainers.junit.jupiter.Testcontainers;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@DataJpaTest
@Testcontainers
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
public class AccountRepositoryTest {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @Autowired
    private AccountRepository accountRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldSaveAndFindAccount() {
        Account account = new Account("SAV2001", "CUST001", "SAVINGS",
                new BigDecimal("150000.00"));
        accountRepository.save(account);

        Account found = accountRepository.findById("SAV2001").orElseThrow();
        assertThat(found.getBalance()).isEqualByComparingTo(new BigDecimal("150000.00"));
    }
}
```

```text
Test execution:
1. Testcontainers starts a PostgreSQL 16 Docker container.
2. @ServiceConnection auto-configures spring.datasource.url to point at the container.
3. Flyway migrations run against the real PostgreSQL.
4. Test executes against real PostgreSQL (not H2).
5. Container is destroyed after tests complete.
```

### What is @Value and SpEL?

@Value injects a single property from application.yml. SpEL (Spring Expression Language) allows evaluating dynamic expressions inside @Value.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class LoanConfigService {

    // Simple property injection
    @Value("${loan.max-amount:5000000}")
    private BigDecimal maxLoanAmount;

    // SpEL expression -- evaluates at runtime
    @Value("#{${loan.base-rate} + ${loan.margin}}")
    private BigDecimal effectiveRate;

    // System property
    @Value("#{systemProperties['user.timezone']}")
    private String timezone;

    public boolean isEligible(BigDecimal requestedAmount) {
        return requestedAmount.compareTo(maxLoanAmount) <= 0;
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml
loan:
  max-amount: 5000000
  base-rate: 8.5
  margin: 1.25
```

```text
effectiveRate = 8.5 + 1.25 = 9.75
maxLoanAmount = 5000000
timezone = Asia/Kolkata
```

### What is @Lazy and when should you use it?

@Lazy defers bean creation until the bean is first used. By default, Spring creates all singleton beans at startup. This is fine for most beans, but some beans are expensive to create (like a connection to an external system that might not be needed).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Lazy;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
@Lazy  // created only when first injected or called
public class ReportGenerationService {

    public ReportGenerationService() {
        // expensive initialization: connects to external reporting engine
        System.out.println("ReportGenerationService initialized");
    }

    public byte[] generateStatement(String accountId) {
        // generate PDF statement for account SAV2001
        return new byte[0];
    }
}
```

```text
Without @Lazy:
  Startup: ReportGenerationService initialized (even if no one calls it)

With @Lazy:
  Startup: nothing happens
  First call to generateStatement(): ReportGenerationService initialized
```

### What are @Conditional annotations?

@Conditional annotations control whether a bean is created based on conditions. Spring Boot provides several built-in variants.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnProperty;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnMissingBean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class CacheConfig {

    // Only create this bean if the property is set to true
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "cache.redis.enabled", havingValue = "true")
    public CacheManager redisCacheManager() {
        return new RedisCacheManager(); // real Redis cache
    }

    // Create this bean only if no other CacheManager exists
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean(CacheManager.class)
    public CacheManager inMemoryCacheManager() {
        return new ConcurrentMapCacheManager(); // simple in-memory fallback
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml
cache:
  redis:
    enabled: true   # uses RedisCacheManager
    # enabled: false  # falls back to ConcurrentMapCacheManager
```

```text
With cache.redis.enabled=true:
  RedisCacheManager bean is created.
  ConditionalOnMissingBean: CacheManager exists, so inMemoryCacheManager is NOT created.

With cache.redis.enabled=false:
  ConditionalOnProperty: redisCacheManager is NOT created.
  ConditionalOnMissingBean: no CacheManager exists, so inMemoryCacheManager IS created.
```

### What are common mistakes with these features?

The most common mistake with @Scheduled is forgetting @EnableScheduling. Without it, all @Scheduled annotations are completely ignored.

```java
// WRONG: missing @EnableScheduling -- method never runs
@Component
public class InterestScheduler {
    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void calculateInterest() { /* never executes */ }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: @EnableScheduling activates the scheduler
@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
public class SchedulingConfig { }

@Component
public class InterestScheduler {
    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void calculateInterest() { /* runs at 2 AM daily */ }
}
```

With Flyway, a common mistake is modifying a migration file after it has been applied. Flyway checks the checksum of each file. If the checksum changes, startup fails with a validation error.

```text
Flyway error:
Migration checksum mismatch for migration V2__add_balance_column.sql
  Applied checksum: 1234567890
  Resolved checksum: 9876543210

Fix: never modify applied migrations. Create V5__fix_balance_default.sql instead.
```

With @Retryable, forgetting @EnableRetry causes all retry annotations to be silently ignored.

### What is CommandLineRunner vs ApplicationRunner?

Both run once after the application context is fully initialized. The difference is the argument type: CommandLineRunner receives raw String[] args, while ApplicationRunner receives a parsed ApplicationArguments object.

Rajesh uses CommandLineRunner to load the holiday calendar into cache on startup.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import com.bank.service.HolidayService;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

@Configuration
public class StartupConfig {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StartupConfig.class);

    @Bean
    CommandLineRunner loadHolidayCalendar(HolidayService holidayService) {
        return args -> {
            int count = holidayService.loadCalendar();
            log.info("Loaded {} holidays into cache", count);
        };
    }

    @Bean
    CommandLineRunner verifyDatabaseConnectivity(javax.sql.DataSource dataSource) {
        return args -> {
            try (var conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
                log.info("Database connectivity verified: {}", conn.getMetaData().getURL());
            }
        };
    }
}
```

```text
Startup log:
Started NbiBankingApplication in 3.2 seconds
Loaded 52 holidays into cache
Database connectivity verified: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/nbi_banking
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Multipart file upload safety.** Validate the file type, enforce a maximum size with `spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size`, and stream the file to storage (S3, MinIO) without loading the entire file into memory. Never trust the client-provided filename.
- **Spring DevTools.** Enables automatic restart on code changes during development and a live-reload trigger for the browser. It should never be on the production classpath.
- **GraalVM native images.** Ahead-of-time compilation eliminates JVM startup entirely (0.01s startup). Limitations: no runtime reflection without configuration, no dynamic class loading. Add `@RegisterReflectionForBinding` for DTOs that need reflection.
- **BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor vs BeanPostProcessor.** BeanDefinitionRegistryPostProcessor modifies bean definitions before any bean is created (e.g., registering beans programmatically). BeanPostProcessor modifies bean instances after creation.
- **Spring State Machine.** Models complex business workflows with explicit states and transitions. At National Bank of India, a loan application moves through states: SUBMITTED -> UNDER_REVIEW -> APPROVED -> DISBURSED (or REJECTED). The state machine enforces valid transitions.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the difference between fixedRate and fixedDelay if the task takes longer than the interval? (fixedRate: next execution starts immediately after the previous one finishes because it was already overdue. fixedDelay: always waits the full delay after the previous execution completes.)
- Can @Retryable retry on multiple exception types? (Yes, use retryFor = {ConnectException.class, TimeoutException.class}.)
- What happens if you run two instances of the same service with @Scheduled? (Both instances execute the scheduled method independently. Use ShedLock or a database lock to ensure only one instance runs the task.)
- How does Testcontainers manage container lifecycle? (With @Container, the container starts before tests and stops after. Static containers are shared across all tests in the class; instance containers are recreated per test method.)
- What is the difference between @Value and @ConfigurationProperties? (@Value injects one property at a time. @ConfigurationProperties binds an entire group of properties to a POJO, with type safety and validation.)
